{
  "assessment": "The source supports the incompressible kinetic energy E_i scaling with quench duration tau_Q at two temperatures (T=10^-6 and T=1), with fitted power laws and explicit fit uncertainties. The operational definition of equilibration time is provided in the supplementary material. The control tau_Q is a linear chemical-potential ramp with dimensionless scaled units. The readout E_i is defined via the density-weighted velocity field and its incompressible component. The claims are anchored to the relevant lines, including the fit method and ensemble counts. The applicability domain [100,1000] is supported by reviewer figure evidence and the source's own fit ranges. The fast-quench deviation statement is noted as a general limitation, not part of the fitted domain. The paper scope is revised to avoid conflating scaling with collapse. The fit uncertainty convention is source_unspecified, and the numeric values are preserved. The card is case_authoring_ready true.",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "Seong-Ho Shinn, Spontaneous Quantum Turbulence in a Newborn Bose-Einstein Condensate via the Kibble-Zurek Mechanism, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/b16v-hwp2",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/b16v-hwp2",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2506.21670v3"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper establishes the Kibble-Zurek universality of the total incompressible kinetic energy E_i at equilibration time, reporting fitted power-law scalings with quench duration tau_Q at two temperatures (T=10^-6 and T=1). The separate collapse of rescaled spectra is not part of this selected cluster.",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "kzm_ei_scaling",
      "statement": "The incompressible kinetic energy at equilibration scales as E_i proportional to tau_Q^{-(1+2 nu)/(1+z nu)}, with mean-field exponents nu=1/2 and z=2, giving E_i proportional to tau_Q^{-1}.",
      "equations": [
        "E_i \\propto \\tau_Q^{-(1+2\\nu)/(1+z\\nu)}",
        "E_i = A \\tau_Q^{-\\alpha}"
      ],
      "variable_names": ["E_i", "tau_Q", "nu", "z", "A", "alpha"],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "nu_critical_exponent",
          "value": 0.5,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "correlation-length critical exponent",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 167,
              "line_end": 167,
              "support": "Using the mean-field values of the critical exponents nu = 1/2 and z = 2"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "z_dynamic_exponent",
          "value": 2,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "dynamic critical exponent",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 167,
              "line_end": 167,
              "support": "Using the mean-field values of the critical exponents nu = 1/2 and z = 2"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "ei_amplitude_lowT",
          "value": 197740,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified: +/- 5.010e4",
          "unit": "dimensionless (scaled units)",
          "role": "amplitude of the fitted power law for E_i at T=10^-6",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 443,
              "line_end": 443,
              "support": "E_i = (19.774 ± 5.010) × 10^4 τ_Q^{-0.971 ± 0.052} for panel (a)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "ei_exponent_lowT",
          "value": -0.971,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified: +/- 0.052",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "exponent of the fitted power law for E_i at T=10^-6",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 443,
              "line_end": 443,
              "support": "E_i = (19.774 ± 5.010) × 10^4 τ_Q^{-0.971 ± 0.052} for panel (a)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "ei_amplitude_T1",
          "value": 95430,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified: +/- 2.951e4",
          "unit": "dimensionless (scaled units)",
          "role": "amplitude of the fitted power law for E_i at T=1",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 443,
              "line_end": 443,
              "support": "E_i = (9.543 ± 2.951) × 10^4 τ_Q^{-0.935 ± 0.068} in panel (b)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "ei_exponent_T1",
          "value": -0.935,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified: +/- 0.068",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "exponent of the fitted power law for E_i at T=1",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 443,
              "line_end": 443,
              "support": "E_i = (9.543 ± 2.951) × 10^4 τ_Q^{-0.935 ± 0.068} in panel (b)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 414,
          "line_end": 417,
          "support": "Equation (eq:scaling_E_i_nu_z): E_i proportional to tau_Q^{-(1+2 nu)/(1+z nu)}"
        },
        {
          "line_start": 443,
          "line_end": 444,
          "support": "Fitted power laws confirm the predicted tau_Q^{-1} behavior based on mean-field exponents nu=1/2 and z=2."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "fixed_conditions": [
    {
      "condition_id": "simulation_parameters_common",
      "statement": "Periodic L x L homogeneous box with L=30, initial chemical potential mu_i=0.1, final chemical potential mu_f=20, coupling g=1, damping gamma=0.03, energy cutoff epsilon_cut=2.5 mu_f, and relaxation time t_r=10. Length, time, and temperature are measured in units of l=sqrt(hbar^2/(m E_sc)), hbar/E_sc, and E_sc/k_B respectively.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 460,
          "line_end": 460,
          "support": "Length, time, and temperature are measured in units of l, hbar/E_sc, and E_sc/k_B."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 491,
          "line_end": 507,
          "support": "Simulations in periodic L x L box with L=30, t_r=10, mu_i=0.1, mu_f=20, g=1, gamma=0.03, T=1, epsilon_cut=2.5 mu_f."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "equilibration_time_definition",
      "statement": "The equilibration time t_eq is determined by analyzing N_C/L^2, locating the global maximum of its second derivative, then identifying the subsequent interval (t1,t2) where the second derivative is <= -Delta with Delta=10% of that maximum, and setting t_eq=t2.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 514,
          "line_end": 517,
          "support": "The equilibration time t_eq is identified by analyzing N_C/L^2, locating the global maximum of d^2(N_C/L^2)/dt^2, and identifying the subsequent interval (t1,t2) where d^2(N_C/L^2)/dt^2 <= -Delta with Delta=10% of the maximum, then taking t_eq=t2."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "temperature_lowT",
      "statement": "Temperature T = 10^-6 (dimensionless scaled units).",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 390,
          "line_end": 390,
          "support": "Panels (a) and (b) show the incompressible kinetic energy E_i at the equilibration time for various quench times tau_Q at temperature T = 10^-6 and T = 1, respectively."
